 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T05:15+08:00",
 "answer": "Singapore's air quality is currently **very unhealthy**, because of haze from regional forest fires. These are the latest figures I found, and they may have changed since.\n\n**Latest reports (Friday, Oct 16)**\n- Central Singapore's 24-hour PSI went into the very unhealthy range at midnight. Business Today reported it at 227 at 9am, the highest so far this year. One report, citing Channel NewsAsia, put it at 229 at 3pm.\n- At 10am, the east and west were also very unhealthy, at 202 and 201. The north was at 150 and the south at 183, both unhealthy.\n- Singapore has had nearly two weeks of unhealthy air in at least part of the island. The last time all five regions were moderate at once was Oct 5.\n- A live tracker, schlah.com, showed Central at 219 (very unhealthy) for the 24-hour PSI. The 1-hour PM2.5 was 136, in the \"elevated\" band.\n\n**What to do**\n- Limit prolonged or strenuous outdoor activity.\n- Wear an N95 mask if you have to be outside.\n- Keep windows closed.\n- People with heart or lung conditions, older adults, children and pregnant women should be especially careful.\n\nFor the live reading, check the NEA website or the myENV app. They update hourly, and the 1-hour PM2.5 is the better guide for what you're doing in the next hour.\n\nSources: Business Today, News.az, schlah.com, checkpointsg.com",
